Spec: Spec fix replaces software-configurable LMUL with hardware-derived VLENE in the C accumulator register group multiplier formula and mandates CMUL-aligned starting register indices, preventing silent vector register file aliasing during integrated matrix multiply-accumulate.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_v_ldst.c:24-75  set_vreg
      Register-file byte offset computed as (which % 8) * vlenb with which/8 bank selection (v0/v8/v16/v24). Under Zvvmm/Zvvfmm matrix mode, the C accumulator register group uses CMUL = LMUL/λ² for register-to-bank mapping and requires CMUL-aligned starting register index. This code uses a fixed 8-register-per-bank layout and has no matrix-mode awareness. If called while vtype is matrix-configured, writes will land in the wrong vector register bank, enabling tile overlap and silent data corruption. The invariant 'vtype is never in matrix mode' is not enforced locally.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_v_ldst.c:77-128  get_vreg
      Identical register-file byte-offset assumption as set_vreg: (which % 8) * vlenb with which/8 bank selection. When reading matrix operand tiles (A, B) or accumulator (C) from the register file under matrix-mode vtype, the register-index-to-bank mapping is incorrect because it ignores CMUL = LMUL/λ² group multiplier and CMUL-alignment constraints. Silent read of wrong data. No matrix-mode guard exists locally.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_v_ldst.c:130-138  vsetvl
      Inline function writes vl and vtype via CSR write with zero validation. Used to restore vl/vtype after emulation completes (lines 219, 235, 318, 329). If vtype was in matrix mode, this blindly restores a matrix-configured vtype without checking CMUL alignment or matrix-mode constraints. While the emulator's internal vsetvli (e8, m8) in set_vreg/get_vreg temporarily overwrites vtype for its own use, the final vsetvl restoration propagates whatever vtype S-mode had — including matrix-mode values — back into hardware without any firmware-level validation.
